Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
like many of the best professional  photographers flo ngala finds herself at  the intersection of art  and photojournalism over the last few  years a 26 year old photographed some of  the world's most iconic celebrities  but when americans took to the streets  in the wake of george floyd's murder  and gala was inspired to capture those  scenes those  images taken in her hometown of new york  city draw from the street photography  and portraits  that stretch back to the start of her  career  on my way to school i would have my  camera with me literally these streets  like anyone who looked beautiful  growing up in her harlem neighborhood  flo and gala quickly discovered the  beauty  all around her and more than a decade  ago began  documenting it with her camera how old  were you when you were taking these you  think  i was like 15. and gala met us at a  neighborhood pub  it was once home to a store run by her  parents  who immigrated from west africa in 1992.  they owned a hair rating and beauty  supply store it was very much a family  business  like the first exposure i had to a black  owned business which was very powerful  it was really like an immigrant story of  like just  hard work and perseverance harlem is  like this beautiful mecca of black  excellence  seeing that and being inspired by that  is what was ingrained in me  at a very very young age they were just  fearless and resilient so that's where i  got that from  her parents along with a high school  photography teacher encouraged her to  get creative  we got really close and i think that  created a very safe space for me to  create  she also was a very like sharp woman and  that's kind of how i learned i think for  sure just like the tough love of it  my dad passed away in 2008 and so he  actually never saw me become a  photographer but i do feel like  my entry into photography happened very  soon after his passing  i remember i was always attracted to  people who were deep in thought  seeing things like on the subway you  know that's the actual best place to see  all parts of new york city  she began with self-portraits and street  photography  then in college she was cast as an extra  in a fat joe music video  it wasn't her work on screen but behind  the camera that  led to her big break i emailed the  photos to the director and the producer  he sent those photos to someone that he  works with at atlantic records  a marketing executive at the record  company liked what she saw  and offered her an opportunity to  photograph rapper gucci mane after he  left prison in 2016.  the following year she captured cardi  b's assent to fame and gala  was only 22. i always really believed in  myself and ultimately that's what i  think  has manifested itself into exactly what  i thought i wanted to do  at 15. she has since photographed  various celebrities from rapper busta  rhymes to actress leticia wright  and politician stacey abrams and landed  covers on billboard  and the new york times and gala's main  mission behind the camera she says  is to tell the truth with somebody like  cardi b  what do you see i still see her as a  woman of color from the bronx  who like got out the mud like that's  exactly how i see her  and i know exactly what that feels like  and looks like because i did it  my mom did it and so there's no kind of  i think  narrative i'm imposing on her when  demonstrations swept new york a little  more than a year ago following george  floyd's death  and gala took to the streets first to  protest  then to photograph he was murdered the  same week as my 25th birthday i just was  like well there's  no way i can just sit here and if it  wasn't for photography there were so  many things that we would have never  been able to see and those things were  the truth that's how things were at the  time that's how people felt them to be  so i think that's what really felt  powerful to me  and gala is also an alumni of the  organization figure skating in harlem  which you will recall we featured right  here on cbs this morning back in march  wow yeah she told me that it's a  blessing to be an alumna  and that sisterhood and the community of  the program fostered  her own personal development she started  she do  honestly she's a figure skater fantastic  i love the shot you just showed the  black and white with the hand raised and  this  guy with the mask really strong  photography and her personality  she looks i mean that's one of the  things we talked about when she's  photographing  she searches for the truth in the image  that she frames in her mind you know  what i like she captures moments  very candid that she just captures it  right when it's happening absolutely  she's also she's been she's had a couple  of opportunities and she's really seized  them that's really and she's only 26  years old  i just go ahead i was well i love that  it's her photography teacher from high  school that really encouraged  her and sent her on that props to that  photography teacher for  inspiring her and we talked a little bit  about those that came before her like  gordon parks who also  photographed really powerful scenes in  harlem um when he was a photographer for  life magazine  and she's inspired by that again another  example of a teacher that sees something  in you that you don't even see yourself  sometimes that's important  one teacher can change your life thank  you vlad

